course: everyday
lesson: 362
title: Spending money online without asking
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Aiden in Phoenix bought gems in his favourite game with Mum's saved card, a few dollars at a time, so he could beat his friend. Each tap felt like nothing. At the end of the month Mum saw over forty dollars gone from her bank account and sat down at the kitchen table very quietly. Aiden owned up. He said sorry and did extra jobs for weeks to pay it back, and the card was removed from the game.
explain: Sophie said she was thirteen so she could join an app her friends used. She saw videos that scared her and could not stop thinking about them at night. When Mum found out, she deleted the app — and instead of shouting, she sat with Sophie and talked about what she had seen. Sophie felt better once she had told.
explain: In-app money is real money: gems, coins and skins are paid for from a real bank account. Age rules on apps exist to keep children away from things that can upset or harm them.
explain: Behind every screen is a real person — the classmate in the chat, the friend in the photo, the person who made the video. Online words and pictures can hurt just as much as spoken ones, and they can spread and stay.
fact: Money spent in games and apps comes from a real bank account.
fact: Many apps and social networks require users to be at least 13 years old.
fact: If you see something online that scares or upsets you, tell a trusted adult.
fact: Mean messages in a group chat can hurt as much as mean words in the playground.
fact: Sharing a photo of someone without asking can embarrass them, and it can spread beyond your control.
fact: Pop-ups saying you have won a prize are usually tricks to get information or install harmful software, such as a virus that damages the computer.
fact: Never tell someone you have only met online where you live or go to school.
fact: Screens late at night make it harder to fall asleep and leave you tired the next day.
fact: Using someone's phone without asking can drain their battery when they need it, so they may miss an important call.
fact: Owning up quickly about online mistakes lets adults fix them faster.
exercise: Why did Aiden buy gems with Mum's card? | He wanted to win against his friend | Mum said he could buy anything | The game said gems were free | He thought it was his own card
exercise: Why did each purchase feel like nothing to Aiden? | Tapping did not feel like spending real money | The gems were really free, so it was not spending | He used play money | The game paid it back
exercise: What put Aiden's spending right? | Owning up and doing jobs to pay it back | Deleting the game to hide it | Saying his friend did it | Buying more gems to win faster
exercise: Sophie said she was thirteen to join an app. What happened? | She saw videos that scared her | She made lots of new safe friends | Nothing, the app was fine | She won a prize
exercise: Why do apps have age rules? | To keep children from content that can upset or harm them | To make younger children feel left out on purpose | Because older people type faster | They are only for show
exercise: What helped Sophie most after the scary videos? | Telling Mum and talking about what she saw | Watching more videos until she got used to them | Keeping it secret from everyone | Joining a different app
exercise: Max sent a mean message to a classmate in the group chat. The boy showed his mum. What put it right? | Saying sorry to him in person | Deleting the chat and saying nothing | Saying it was a joke | Sending a funny message after
exercise: Ella shared a funny photo of her friend without asking. Her friend was embarrassed. What should Ella have done? | Asked her friend first | Shared it with fewer people | Added a funny caption to it | Shared it only on weekends
exercise: Leo clicked on a pop-up saying he had won a prize. What happened? | The computer got a virus | He won a real prize, which arrived in the post | Nothing happened at all | The internet got faster
exercise: What did Leo do right after the pop-up? | Told Dad straight away | Clicked it again to check | Hid it from Dad for a while | Turned the computer off forever
exercise: Mia told an online friend where she lives. Why was Mum scared? | She did not know who the online friend really was | Mia's address is a secret from friends | Online friends never come to visit, so it was safe | Mum did not want visitors
exercise: Jack watched videos past bedtime on his tablet. What was the consequence? | He was tired and grumpy at school the next day | He did better at school | He slept much better after the videos and felt rested | Nothing, screens help sleep
exercise: Kai left a mean comment on a video. The maker replied that it hurt them. What did Kai learn? | There is a real person behind every screen | Comments never reach anyone | Video makers really like getting mean comments | It is fine if you don't use your name
exercise: Ruby played games on Dad's phone without asking and the battery died. What happened? | Dad missed an important call | Dad's phone charged up faster | Dad thanked her for testing it | Nothing happened to Dad at all
exercise: Aiden's friend says «I buy stuff on my mum's card all the time and she never checks». What is true? | It is still real money taken without asking | If she never checks the card, then it's free | Game money isn't real | It's fine under ten dollars
test: «it's only 99p, mum won't notice». You're about to buy coins on her phone. What's true? | It's real money, and you need to ask first | 99p isn't really real money, so it doesn't matter | It's free if it's under £1 | Coins pay themselves back
test: «just say ur 13, everyone does». What's the wise answer? | No, the age rule is there for a reason | OK, nobody ever checks how old you are anyway | Say you're 18 to be safe | Use your sister's name
test: Pop-up: «CONGRATULATIONS!!! You've won an iPhone! Click here!!» What do you do? | Close it and tell an adult | Click on it quickly to claim your prize | Enter your home address to win | Share it with all your friends
test: online «friend» in a game: «where u live? what school u go to?». Best move? | Don't say, and tell an adult | Tell him just the town, not the street | Tell him the name of your school only | Send him a photo of yourself instead
test: bought gems last month and Mum never said anything, «it worked last time, so it's fine». Is it? | No, it was real money taken without asking | Yes, if she didn't notice last month then it's fine | Yes, gems are free | Only on her birthday
test: group chat: «lol look at Ben's face» with a photo. Everyone's posting laughing emojis. You didn't post it, but you could add one. What's right? | Don't join in; tell the sender to take it down | Add just one laughing emoji, it's harmless | Share it with another chat | Post a worse photo
test: Mum: «Why is there £45 gone from my account to GameStore??» It was you. Best reply? | «It was me. I'm sorry. I'll pay it back» | «Must be a hacker» | «The game took it by itself, I didn't do anything» | «I only spent a bit»
test: «saw something on that app and now i can't sleep». What's the best advice? | Tell a parent; it helps to talk | Watch it again so you get used to it | Keep it secret | Delete your memory
test: Dad's phone's at 5%, you wanna play. Dad's waiting for a call from work. Best move? | Ask Dad first and leave it if he says no | Play on it until the battery dies | Play with the sound off | Turn on airplane mode
test: «I left a comment saying her video's trash, she's just some random». What's true? | She's a real person and it can hurt her | Randoms on the internet don't really have feelings | It's fine if she can't see you | Comments disappear at once
test: midnight, still on the tablet «just one more video». What happens tomorrow? | You're tired at school | You're full of energy | Videos make you sleep less but feel better | Nothing, sleep doesn't matter
exam: Identify the principal reason for which purchases made within a game are to be regarded as expenditure of real money. | The sums are debited from a genuine bank account | Such purchases are invariably refunded in full to the account holder | In-game currency has no monetary origin | They are paid for by the game's developer
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the age restrictions imposed by many applications? | They exist to protect children from content that may upset or harm them | They exist solely in order to exclude younger users from enjoyment | They exist only to collect users' personal data | They may be disregarded with parental absence
exam: It is given that a child has been informed by a pop-up notice that a prize has been won. Select the response consistent with safe conduct. | The notice is closed and a trusted adult informed | The notice is followed and personal details supplied | The notice is forwarded to acquaintances | The notice is followed only once
exam: State the course of action most consistent with prudence where a person met only online requests a child's home address. | The address is withheld and a trusted adult informed | The address is supplied if the person seems friendly | Only the street is supplied | The address is supplied after a week of acquaintance
